start-cosmic assumes XDG_RUNTIME_DIR already exists, created earlier by
pam_systemd/pam_elogind during login. That holds when COSMIC is started
from a greeter (cosmic-greeter, lightdm, ...) since the greeter's PAM
session sets it up first. It does NOT hold when start-cosmic is invoked
from a shell profile (e.g. ~/.zprofile) on a manual TTY login, if
elogind hasn't finished registering the session and creating
/run/user/$UID yet -- cosmic-comp then panics (exit code 101) trying to
create its Wayland socket in a directory that doesn't exist, and
restart-loops silently since .zprofile doesn't surface stderr anywhere
visible. Reported as a hang with a blinking cursor:
https://codeberg.org/Bella109/void-packages/issues/7

Bootstrap XDG_RUNTIME_DIR ourselves if it's unset or missing, instead of
trusting that something else already created it. This is a no-op on
setups where a greeter already did the job correctly.

--- a/data/start-cosmic
+++ b/data/start-cosmic
@@ -3,6 +3,13 @@
 set -e

 # From: https://people.debian.org/~mpitt/systemd.conf-2016-graphical-session.pdf
+
+# Bootstrap XDG_RUNTIME_DIR if whatever started this session (e.g. a
+# manual TTY login via ~/.zprofile, without a greeter) didn't already
+# set it up via pam_elogind/pam_systemd. cosmic-comp panics if this
+# directory doesn't exist when it tries to create its Wayland socket.
+export XDG_RUNTIME_DIR="${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR:-/run/user/$(id -u)}"
+mkdir -p -m 0700 "${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR}"

 if command -v systemctl >/dev/null; then
     # robustness: if the previous graphical session left some failed units,
